Given \(V_{BE}=0.6\) V and \(\beta = 150\). Given circuit is, <img src="img/2a1b55c18db317a6.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> To determine the type of feedback, assume that the op-amp output voltage increases slightly. Then <ul><li>Base voltage increases.</li><li>Collector current \(I_{C}\) increases.</li><li>Voltage drop across the \(5\,\text{k}\Omega\) resistor increases.</li><li>Collector voltage \(V_{C}\) decreases.</li><li>Since \(V_{C}\) is connected to the non-inverting input, \(V_{+}\) decreases.</li><li>The op-amp responds by decreasing its output voltage.</li></ul> Hence, the circuit opposes the original change, indicating negative feedback. Since the op-amp is ideal, \[V_{+}=V_{-}=5\,\text{V}.\] Therefore, \(V_{C}=5\,\text{V}\) The collector current is \[I_{C}=\frac{10-5}{5\,\text{k}\Omega}=1\,\text{mA}.\] \[I_{B}=\frac{I_{C}}{\beta}
=\frac{1\,\text{mA}}{150}
=6.67\,\mu\text{A}.\] Hence, \[I_{E}=I_{C}+I_{B}
=1.0067\,\text{mA}.\] The emitter voltage is \[V_{E}=I_{E}\times1.4\,\text{k}\Omega
=1.0067\,\text{mA}\times1.4\,\text{k}\Omega
\approx1.41\,\text{V}.\] the base voltage (which is also the op-amp output) is \[V=V_{B}=V_{E}+V_{BE}
=1.41+0.6
\approx2.01\,\text{V}
\approx2\,\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(D)\quad Negative feedback, }V=2\,\text{V}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not assume positive feedback merely because the collector is connected to the non-inverting input; always check the effect of a small increase in the output.</li></ul></div>

