From the magnitude plot, gain is high at low frequencies. As frequency increases, the magnitude decreases. Hence, the network initially behaves like a lag compensator. In the mid-frequency region, magnitude remains almost constant at a low value. As frequency increases further, magnitude starts increasing. Hence, the network now behaves like a lead compensator. Therefore, the overall characteristic is: \[\text{Lag action}
\rightarrow
\text{Lead action}\] Thus, the compensator is a lag-lead compensator. However, this option is not explicitly present in the given choices. \[\boxed{\text{Lag-lead compensator}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>Lag compensator attenuates gain with increasing frequency.</li><li>Lead compensator increases gain with increasing frequency.</li><li>Sequence of magnitude variation determines whether the network is lead-lag or lag-lead.</li></ul></div>

